import { ExplanationCopy } from "../../explanation"
import { ReferenceCopy } from "../../reference-article"

pub fn copy -> ReferenceCopy = ReferenceCopy {
  title: "Cancellation and resources",
  summary: (explanation ()).summary,
  ruleTitle: "Release acquired resources when their work ends",
  ruleBody: "Files and connections need release after acquisition. bracket receives acquisition, use, and release operations and releases after use ends. acquireRelease registers cleanup for the acquired value in the current scope. A scope is the boundary managing work and resources. Cancellation between successful acquisition and cleanup registration cannot leave the resource without release.",
  typingBody: "Cancellation is not a value of Effect's E. A resource release function can inspect success, recoverable failure, or cancellation through EffectExit<E, A>. Cleanup cannot return a recoverable failure to its caller, so its error type must be Never.",
  evaluationBody: "When a scope ends, it requests cancellation from unfinished children and waits for cleanup. Registered finalizers run once in reverse registration order. New cancellation is deferred during finalization. If explicit release and scope release overlap, the same resource is not released twice.",
  invalidTitle: "Handle release failures inside cleanup",
  invalidBody: "Handle anticipated release failures inside the finalizer, using recover or a similar operation. If one finalizer defects, the runner attempts the remaining finalizers before reporting the first defect. Later defects remain in diagnostics. A finalizer defect takes reporting precedence over the original computation's recoverable failure.",
  diagnosticsBody: "Identify whether acquisition, use, or release failed. A finalizer that never completes also prevents its scope from finishing. Distinguish the original termination reason from any defects during cleanup.",
}

pub fn explanation -> ExplanationCopy = ExplanationCopy {
  question: "How do I clean up after both success and failure?",
  summary: "A resource needs release when its use ends. scoped creates a managed region, and acquireRelease connects acquisition with cleanup so the region cleans up before returning.",
  reading: "The example opens no files: it records acquiring and releasing names A, B and C. trace is a Ref holding the record string. acquire treats a name as the acquired value and registers a cleanup function that appends the name to trace.",
  result: "The first scope succeeds with 42. It releases the later acquisition B before A, recording BA. The next acquires C and calls failWith with expected. failWith succeeds with ok for an empty reason, and otherwise fails with that reason. Cleanup finishes before recover turns the reason into a success value, leaving BAC."
}

pub fn readerBridgeOne -> String =
  "The imports provide effects and refs. After the name and trace parameters, Task<String> states that acquire produces a procedure succeeding with the name. \\released: String -> ... is the cleanup function; its inner \\text: String -> text + released updates the record. In main, <- receives a success value and _ <- waits without keeping it. effects.scoped $ do { ... } passes the whole do block as one argument, just like effects.scoped (do { ... }). |> passes the procedure value on its left as the last argument of the call on its right. Here it passes the scoped procedure to recover, attaching the response to a failure."

pub fn readerBridgeTwo -> String =
  "The cleanup function is a finalizer; the managed region is a scope. Within one scope, finalizers run once, with the latest registered first. Cancellation uses the same rule. It requests that work stop and is distinct from a typed failure value. Successful acquisition and finalizer registration have an atomic handoff so cancellation cannot lose the resource between them."

pub fn readerMistake -> String =
  "The rejected finalizer leaves a typed failure. Handle required failures within cleanup so its error type is Never. A finalizer defect still allows the remaining cleanup attempts, but an endlessly running finalizer or forced process termination prevents a completion guarantee."

pub fn readerCaption -> String = "Record acquisition and cleanup to inspect their order"

pub fn readerInvalidCaption -> String = "A declaration or call to correct"

pub fn readerDetailNote -> String =
  "The following declaration-only fragment is a smaller view of the same topic. It has no entry point or output."

pub fn readerLegacyReading -> String =
  "The example uses mutable state in a Ref to observe cleanup. released starts False. scoped creates an inner management scope. Inside it, acquireRelease registers refs.set True released as cleanup, which runs when that scope ends."

pub fn readerLegacyResult -> String =
  "useScoped returns True because it reads released after the scope ends and observes that cleanup ran. For an actual file, register the operation closing its acquired handle. This registered cleanup is called a finalizer."
